{:schema :library-contract/decisions-v1
 :decisions
 [{:id :spider-editorial-standing
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-01"
   :question "Gate 0 of P-organise-the-library.md: what is the spider's editorial standing under README-flexiarg §5a?"
   :grounds "P-organise-the-library.md §2 already defines it consistently with §5a's who-writes column: the spider is an EDITOR, so it may write @how and @see-also, and may only PROPOSE @why (the author-written, causal directive). Not an arm choice: any stronger standing would contradict §5a's table; any weaker would make the fleet pointless. Recorded so Joe can veto rather than pre-approve (rule of 2026-09-01)."
   :veto "Joe may narrow (propose-only for all kinds) or void; wave-3 output is all :proposed/:attested through review, so a veto costs re-labelling, not re-work."}
  {:id :posthoc-why-syntax
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-01"
   :question "README-flexiarg §5a (2026-08-23) asks that a post-hoc @why be marked inline so a reader can tell a trace-hypothesis from an author's causal claim, and does not fix the syntax."
   :arms
   [{:arm :bang-suffix :form "@why! <id>"
     :parser-impact "Every existing @why consumer that matches the literal directive must learn the variant or silently drop the edge: checks/playout_snatch.clj:237 (`re-matches #\"@why (.*)\"`, full-line, so it reads zero) and checks/library_graph_lint.clj:148 (the kind alternation)."
     :greppability "`grep '@why '` misses it entirely -- the mark sits where the space was -- so the habitual search silently under-counts."
     :cost "Silent under-count in both code and grep, and one character is easy to miss in review."}
    {:arm :inline-annotation :form "@why <id> [post-hoc]"
     :parser-impact "Worst of the three: playout_snatch.clj:237 and library_graph_lint.clj:148 both MATCH the line and then read `[post-hoc]` as part of the target list. The lint drops it (it fails target-pattern) but playout_snatch keyword-ises it into the cascade @why graph. A post-hoc edge is counted as authored unless every target-list parser is changed first."
     :greppability "Greppable as @why, which is the problem: the mark is per-line, not per-edge, so a mixed list `@why a/b c/d [post-hoc]` cannot say which target the mark covers."
     :cost "Fails OPEN -- silence looks like an authored edge -- and mixed lists are ambiguous."}
    {:arm :distinct-directive :form "@why-posthoc <id>"
     :parser-impact "Invisible to both existing consumers without any change: verified 2026-09-01 that playout_snatch.clj:237 returns nil on `@why-posthoc war-room/wr-3` and that the pre-LD1 lint alternation `@(why|how|see-also)` did not match it either. Consumers that should treat it as @why-for-cascade-purposes must opt in by name."
     :greppability "`grep '@why-posthoc'` is exact, but the cost runs the other way: `grep '@why'` and `grep '@why\\b'` BOTH match the post-hoc line (verified: 2 hits over a 2-line file, vs 1 for `grep '@why '`), so a habitual bare-@why search over-counts. The reliable authored-only search is `grep '@why '` with the trailing space."
     :cost "A fourth pattern-to-pattern directive alongside a §5a table introduced as declaring two relations, so the standard has to carry the asymmetry in prose; and bare-@why greps over-count."}]
   :interim :distinct-directive
   :why-interim "It fails SAFE where :inline-annotation fails open: a post-hoc edge cannot masquerade as authored in any consumer that has not opted in, and the opt-in is exactly the point -- §5a says the edge should EARN attestation before it counts. :bang-suffix also fails safe but does so by being unfindable to the ordinary grep; the distinct directive is at least exactly searchable. LD1 lands it in the standard and the lint."
   :changes-it "If wave-3 attestation shows post-hoc edges graduating at a rate where the two-directive split costs more churn than it prevents, revisit toward :bang-suffix with a parser update. A bare-@why over-count reaching a recorded number would also reopen it -- that is the arm's own cost showing up as a measurement error, not a style complaint."
   :landed
   {:by :LD1 :at "2026-09-01"
    :standard "README-flexiarg.md §5a -- the interim form, the invisible-until-opt-in property, and what the lint does with it, pointing back here."
    :whitelist "flexiarg-directives.edn :why-posthoc, :status :standard (ingestable and attestable; the SYNTAX is what is interim, not its standing)."
    :lint "checks/library_graph_lint.clj -- edge-kinds still names the three §5a relations; posthoc-why-kind and counted-edge-kinds are new. Parsed at :148, counted as its own key in :edges-by-kind (never summed into :why), reported per section as :patterns-with-outgoing-why-posthoc, and admitted to valid-attestation? so a post-hoc edge can earn its attestation."
    :counted-separately "The authored measures are untouched: :fraction-organised, :patterns-with-outgoing-why and :patterns-in-why-graph all still filter on :why alone, so no section can raise its organised fraction by adding traces."
    :not-in-scope "cycle-failures does NOT admit post-hoc edges -- acyclicity is a law about authority and a post-hoc edge claims none. checks/spider_runner.clj:18 is unchanged, so the spider lane may not propose a @why-posthoc edge; widening the fleet's vocabulary is a separate decision under :spider-editorial-standing. checks/playout_snatch.clj is unchanged by design (it is the opt-in that has not been taken)."}}
  {:id :posthoc-warrant-evidence
   :status :interim-adopted
   :status-note "Adopted on the numbers, not on a ruling asked for in advance. :measured on 2026-09-02 when L9 slice 3 built all three arms behind a flag and ran them on the seven readings (library/.spider/posthoc-warrant-arms-run-2026-09-02.edn); :interim-adopted the same day when L9 slice 4 flipped the default to :pattern-text and wrote the seven edges. Joe's veto is after the fact and is cheap to exercise: the flag stays, so `--posthoc-warrant store` re-runs any section against the old bar unchanged, and the undo is one constant plus seven directives plus seven attestation rows."
   :adopted
   {:at "2026-09-02"
    :by "claude-owner"
    :slice "L9 slice 4"
    :arm :pattern-text-warrant
    :what-changed
    ["checks/library_graph_lint.clj:267 *posthoc-warrant-arm* :store -> :pattern-text, and warrant-arm's nil case with it. Nothing else in the check moved: the two arms differ ONLY in what a :why-posthoc attestation may cite."
     "The seven readings are now directives: five in library/war-room (wr-6, wr-23, wr-18, wr-14, wr-10) and two in library/aif (two-layer-calibration, valuation-reads-the-paperwork), each with a :state :proposed attestation carrying its warrant as :via :pattern-text pointers."]
    :negative-control
    "The committed library run on the OLD arm now FAILS -- war-room exit 1 with 5 :new-edge-without-attestation and 5 :malformed-attestation, aif exit 1 with 2 and 2 (/tmp/L9s4gates/control-store-*.{edn,exit}). So the flip is what makes the sections pass, not something else that moved at the same time; and the old bar is still there to be run."
    :falsifier-run
    "One pointer in the committed war-room record shifted by a single line (wr-6, :line 17 -> 18) makes the section exit 1 with :pattern-text-excerpt-mismatch (/tmp/L9s4gates/mutant-stale-pointer.edn). The check is live on the real records, not only on fixtures."
    :numbers
    "As slice 3 measured, reproduced on the committed library: library-wide :why stays 86, :patterns-in-why-graph stays 97, :why-posthoc 0 -> 7; war-room :fraction-organised 0.32142857142857145 and aif 0.18181818181818182, both unchanged to the last digit. The arm cannot raise a section's organised fraction."
    :test-couplings-repaired
    ["test/library_graph_lint_test.clj:216 live-library-passes asserted the library carried ZERO post-hoc edges. That was a true measurement until today; it is now pinned at 7, with :why 86 and :patterns-in-why-graph 97 asserted beside it so the two numbers move only together."
     "test/library_graph_lint_test.clj:430 asserted (= :store (warrant-arm {})); it now asserts :pattern-text, and a new case asserts :store is still reachable by flag."
     "test/library_graph_lint_test.clj:485 wave-1-attestations-re-audited-under-reflection-rule-v3 compared the 2026-09-01 audit's :attestations-audited against the row count of library/aif/attestations.edn, which the two new rows broke. The audit measured which CITED STORE RECORDS the reflection rule catches, so its scope is the store-citing rows; the count is taken over those, and the remainder is asserted to be pattern-text-only and :why-posthoc-only, so a store-citing row cannot slip out of the audited count."]}
   :run {:at "2026-09-02"
         :by "claude-owner"
         :record "library/.spider/posthoc-warrant-arms-run-2026-09-02.edn"
         :flag "checks/library_graph_lint.clj --posthoc-warrant store|pattern-text (default store)"
         :results {:keep-the-store-bar "7 edges -> 14 failures (7 :malformed-attestation, 7 :new-edge-without-attestation); both sections exit 1"
                   :warrant-outside-attestations "7 edges -> 7 :new-edge-without-attestation and ZERO warrant checks; the linter never reads a warrant on this arm, so a good one and a bare one are the same row to it"
                   :pattern-text-warrant "7 edges, 21 warrant rows -> 0 failures; both sections exit 0"}
         :key-number ":fraction-organised is unchanged to the last digit on the winning arm -- war-room 0.32142857142857145, aif 0.18181818181818182 -- and library-wide :why stays 86 with :patterns-in-why-graph 97, while :why-posthoc goes 0 -> 7. The arm cannot raise a section's organised fraction. That was the property the wave plan needed and it is now measured rather than argued."
         :cost-found-by-running-it "A pattern-text pointer is invalidated by the act of writing the edge it warrants: inserting the directive shifts the source file's lines, and 12 of the 21 rows -- exactly the source-file ones -- went stale on the first run. Detected mechanically, repaired by taking the pointers after the directives are in (the re-pointed probe resolves all 21, 0 not found). It is a real cost the other two arms do not carry, and it is bounded."
         :what-adoption-would-take "Flip the default arm to :pattern-text. Until then a committed @why-posthoc with an authored warrant fails the section gate checks/spider_runner.clj:93 runs, which passes no flag."}
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :L9
   :question "L9's :statement adopts, on an interim basis, that \"the attestation bar for a post-hoc edge is the same evidence-tag machinery spider edges use.\" Slice 1 (war-room) shows that bar cannot be met by an authored warrant. What evidence may a @why-posthoc attestation carry?"
   :grounds "README-flexiarg.md §5a: \"the rationale layer can therefore only ever be authored, never harvested\", measured as zero of 42 missions citing a war-room ruling. checks/library_graph_lint.clj:252 valid-evidence? admits only :via #{:tag :text}, both routes into the futon1b evidence store, and :551 attestation-semantic-failures fails the record unless the excerpt occurs in the store record it names. So the one directive whose warrant is authored by construction is the one whose attestation must be harvested. Demonstrated three ways in library/.spider/posthoc-warrant-blocker-2026-09-02.edn: an authored warrant with a pattern-file pointer is refused twice over (exit 1, :malformed-attestation plus :new-edge-without-attestation), while the same edge carrying a real store id and a real excerpt that says nothing about it -- the spider's own turn-start prompt -- passes (exit 0, :checks [])."
   :arms
   [{:arm :keep-the-store-bar
     :form "no change; a post-hoc edge cites a store record like any other"
     :cost "The rationale layer cannot be authored at all, which contradicts §5a, or it is authored with decorative store citations, which is the facade probe 2c demonstrates. L9 cannot proceed on either reading."}
    {:arm :pattern-text-warrant
     :form ":via :pattern-text with :file and :line; the excerpt is verified to occur in that file at that path"
     :parser-impact "checks/library_graph_lint.clj:252 valid-evidence? widens its :via set for :why-posthoc edges only; :551 attestation-semantic-failures gains a branch that resolves the pointer against the library rather than the store. Verification stays fail-closed and stays mechanical -- the corpus changes, the discipline does not. Spider edges are untouched: O2 still requires a harvested edge to cite text in a record."
     :cost "A second verification corpus in one function, and a reviewer must know which corpus a record was checked against. Mitigated by :via naming it."}
    {:arm :warrant-outside-attestations
     :form "post-hoc warrants live in a sidecar the linter does not read"
     :cost "Fails open: the acceptance's \"no bare edges\" clause is what makes an unwarranted trace visible, and this arm gives it up. It is also what slice 1 did as a HOLDING position, which is why the readings sit in library/war-room/posthoc-readings.edn with no directive written -- a queue, not a resting place."}]
   :recommends :pattern-text-warrant
   :veto "Joe's, after the fact. The earlier note here said :proposed rather than :interim-adopted because this changes what an attestation MEANS, not just how one is spelled -- that is still true, and it is why the arm was built and RUN before being taken rather than argued for. What makes the veto cheap is the flag and the negative control above: `--posthoc-warrant store` reproduces the old bar on demand, and the seven edges are the only thing in the library that depends on the new one."
   :changes-it "If a war-room ruling ever does turn up cited as a relation in a record independent of the spider, the store bar becomes reachable for that edge and the arm choice is worth re-running. Slice 1 re-checked live and found only spider self-text; the finding record names the query set and the record ids."}
  {:id :editorial-why-mislabelled-as-authored
   :status :proposed
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :L9
   :question "Four aif patterns carry a plain @why whose own trailing comment says it is retrospective sense-making and NOT a causal claim (claude-13, 2026-08-23). LD1 gave that thing its own spelling, @why-posthoc, on 2026-09-01. Do the four convert, and if so on what?"
   :edges
   ["library/aif/candidate-pattern-action-space.flexiarg:7 -> war-room/wr-19-tension-must-generate-not-only-rank"
    "library/aif/expected-free-energy-scorecard.flexiarg:7 -> war-room/wr-27-a-loop-is-born-instrumented-for-its-gain"
    "library/aif/no-self-certification.flexiarg:8 -> war-room/wr-25-good-news-gets-the-same-evidence-discipline-as-bad"
    "library/aif/off-continuity-null-discriminates.flexiarg:8 -> war-room/wr-9-bites-empirical-or-logical"]
   :grounds "README-flexiarg.md 5a asks that a post-hoc @why be marked inline so a reader can tell a trace-hypothesis from an author's causal claim, and :posthoc-why-syntax fixed the spelling. These four predate the spelling and are marked only by a prose comment, which LD1's :landed :counted-separately clause cannot read. As plain @why they raise :fraction-organised, sit inside the authored-authority cycle law that LD1 explicitly keeps post-hoc edges out of, and are invisible to :patterns-with-outgoing-why-posthoc. A further defect makes the prose marking worse than nothing: all four comments end `WR-19 did not produce this pattern`, and only one of the four points at wr-19 -- three disclaim an edge they do not name."
   :measured
   "Not asserted. The conversion was run against a scratch copy of the library at /tmp/L9s2probe with the four directives rewritten and nothing else changed, using the same linter invocation the section gate uses. Section aif moves :edges-by-kind from {:why 6 :why-posthoc 0 :how 13 :see-also 45} to {:why 2 :why-posthoc 4 :how 13 :see-also 45}, :patterns-with-outgoing-why 6 -> 2, :patterns-with-outgoing-why-posthoc 0 -> 4, :fraction-organised 0.18181818181818182 -> 0.06060606060606061. Library-wide, :edges-by-kind moves {:why 86 :why-posthoc 0} -> {:why 82 :why-posthoc 4}, and :patterns-in-why-graph 97 -> 94. :why-cycles is 0 before and after, so no cycle result changes today; the cost is that LD1's exemption is not in force for these four, not that it is currently biting. The scratch run exits 1 with exactly four failures, all :new-edge-without-attestation -- the converted edges leave the baseline and have no attestation to re-enter on."
   :arms
   [{:arm :convert-now
     :form "rewrite the four directives to @why-posthoc"
     :cost "Exits 1 on four :new-edge-without-attestation until each has an attestation, and their warrant is claude-13's editorial reading -- an authored warrant, which is the thing :posthoc-warrant-evidence is about. So this arm cannot be taken before that one settles. Also moves numbers the C88 pin and test/library_graph_lint_test.clj assert."}
    {:arm :convert-when-the-warrant-bar-settles
     :form "same rewrite, plus four :proposed attestations carrying claude-13's reading, once a post-hoc warrant can be recorded"
     :cost "One coordinated change touching four pattern files, four attestation records, the C88 pin and the lint test's expected counts. Larger than it looks, and it should be one item of its own rather than a slice of L9."}
    {:arm :leave-and-mark
     :form "leave the directives as @why and record the mislabelling in the readings file only"
     :cost "Fails open in the direction 5a cares about: the library keeps reporting four traces as authored causal claims, and the fraction-organised number keeps counting them. It is what this slice did, as a HOLDING position, not a resting place."}]
   :recommends :convert-when-the-warrant-bar-settles
   :veto "Joe's. Left :proposed and no file was touched: the conversion moves recorded numbers, including ones a pinned test asserts, so it is not a formatting fix the lane may take back cheaply."
   :blocked-by :posthoc-warrant-evidence
   :unblocked
   {:at "2026-09-02"
    :by "L9 slice 4 adopting :posthoc-warrant-evidence arm :pattern-text-warrant"
    :effect "The four attestations are now writable from claude-13's own comment, so the arm choice here has collapsed to scheduling exactly as :changes-it predicted. NOT DONE IN L9: this decision's own :convert-when-the-warrant-bar-settles arm says the conversion should be one item of its own, and it moves numbers L9 slice 4 has just re-pinned (library-wide :why 86 -> 82, :why-posthoc 7 -> 11, :patterns-in-why-graph 97 -> 94, aif :fraction-organised 0.18181818181818182 -> 0.06060606060606061). It needs a worklist row, not a slice."
    :recommends "Open a class W row for the conversion: rewrite the four directives, write four :via :pattern-text attestations from the trailing comments, repair the three comments that disclaim an edge they do not name, and update the three counts in test/library_graph_lint_test.clj:216 in the same commit."}
   :changes-it "If Joe vetoes :posthoc-warrant-evidence, this decision goes back to choosing between :leave-and-mark and dropping the four edges."}
  {:id :authored-edge-attestation-path
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :LA1
   :question "LA1 slice (b) authors six new snatch/ patterns and 17 edges. Its acceptance says \"section lint and attestation discipline as for any authored pattern\", and slice (a) recorded that library/snatch/attestations.edn does not exist and that slice (b) creates it. How does an AUTHOR'S OWN @why/@how earn its place in the graph?"
   :grounds "It cannot be attested, and this is a property of the checker, not an oversight of the author. checks/library_graph_lint.clj:338 valid-attestation? requires a non-empty :evidence vector; :313 evidence-admissible? admits :via :tag and :via :text, both of which resolve to a futon1b evidence-store record; the one authored-warrant shape, :via :pattern-text (:298-311), is admissible ONLY on a @why-posthoc edge and only under the arm adopted by :posthoc-warrant-evidence. So the attestation machinery is the HARVESTED lane's bar (P-validated-R5 law O2), and an author writing their own causal claim has nothing honest to put in :evidence. Measured: the six files fail the section gate with 17 :new-edge-without-attestation and 6 :argument-body-not-in-baseline (/tmp captured, reproduced in the row's :progress)."
   :arms
   [{:arm :baseline-exempt-set
     :form "the new files' body digests and their edges join library/.spider/baseline-edges.edn, with a dated addendum to :measurement-note naming the item and the counts"
     :precedent "Commit 2fbb58a (claude-15, 2026-08-30) took exactly this path for aif/hierarchical-and-temporal-depth and aif/interoceptive-tripwires: \"their digests and 4 @see-also edges join the author-written exempt set (measurement-note addendum)\". The addendum is still the last sentence of the note."
     :cost "The baseline is the thing that makes a spider edit visible, so every addition to it is a small reduction in what the gate can catch. Bounded by being hand-written per file rather than re-snapshotted: a full re-scan would silently absorb any drift anywhere in the library, because body-failures and missing-atts are both filtered to the section under lint. Verified structurally here -- 17 edges added, 0 removed, 0 existing digest changed, all other keys equal."}
    {:arm :widen-the-pattern-text-warrant
     :form "let :via :pattern-text carry an authored @why/@how, not only a @why-posthoc"
     :cost "It would let an author cite their own prose as the evidence for their own edge, which is what :posthoc-warrant-evidence was careful to keep to the one directive whose warrant is authored BY CONSTRUCTION. A @why is a causal claim about the author's own work; a pointer to the sentence making the claim is not independent of it. This arm dissolves the harvested/authored distinction the whole check is built on."}
    {:arm :mint-store-records
     :form "write futon1b evidence records for the new patterns so :via :text resolves"
     :cost "The record would be the author's own text round-tripped through the store, which is the facade the :posthoc-warrant-evidence probe 2c already demonstrated: an edge passes on a real store id whose excerpt says nothing about it. It buys a green gate and no evidence."}]
   :interim :baseline-exempt-set
   :why-interim "It is the only arm that neither weakens the checker nor fakes evidence, and it has a precedent in the file it edits. What it costs is stated in the addendum rather than hidden: six digests and seventeen edges are exempt from the new-edge rule because their author is not the spider."
   :changes-it "A third lane -- author-written edges that a REVIEWER attests -- would replace this. That is a real gap: nothing in the schema records a reviewer's judgement of an authored edge, only of a harvested one. If wave-3 review wants to refuse an authored edge it currently has no row to write, and this decision should be re-run then."
   :veto "Joe's, after the fact, under the 2026-09-01 record-so-Joe-can-veto rule. Cheap to exercise: reverting is one hunk of baseline-edges.edn and the six files."
   :also-found "library/.spider/baseline-edges.edn held 1241 digests against 1247 library files before this change: six math-formalization/*.flexiarg files are in the library and not in the baseline (assemble-a-basis-from-an-independent-supremum, bound-sylow-order-from-faithful-prime-action, collapse-self-normalizing-sylow-via-faithful-prime-action, probe-constant-name-and-signature, recover-radical-generators-from-a-reciprocal-polynomial-root, replace-orbit-combinatorics-by-a-cyclic-module-quotient). Three are committed (83f92b3, 2026-08-31) and three are untracked working-tree files at the time of writing, so the gap is partly a real drift and partly someone else in flight. They would fail :argument-body-not-in-baseline the first time that section is linted. Not touched by LA1; reported for a row of its own."}

  {:id :temperament-grain
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :LA1
   :question "Slice (a) open question 1: which :grain value does a temperament pattern carry, and is the harness's guard convention the mechanism or merely the precedent?"
   :grounds "checks/playout_snatch.clj makes grain a CHECKED CONJUNCT of every guard: every design entry's :if begins (= :design (:grain s)) (:20,22,24,26,28,30) and every play entry's begins (= :play (:grain s)) (:47,53,66,73,79,88,94,103,105,107,109,113). That is what keeps a rule of one grain from firing in a situation of another, and it is what makes the reflective step indexing rather than self-application (slice (a) section 6)."
   :arms
   [{:arm :third-grain-value
     :form ":grain :policy, a third value in the same checked conjunct"
     :cost "One more value to keep in sync across guards, and the guard for it is UNBUILT -- no policy-grain rule runs today, so the conjunct that would enforce the separation exists only for the two grains that do."}
    {:arm :reuse-design
     :form "temperaments are :design-grain rules"
     :cost "Wrong operand. A :design rule edits the arrangement (an InstitutionEdit); a temperament edits the cascade. Collapsing them would let a temperament fire in a design situation, which is the exact confusion the grain conjunct exists to prevent."}
    {:arm :no-grain-key
     :form "distinguish by the rule's type alone (Rule CascadeState CascadeEdit), no grain field"
     :cost "The type is not present at runtime: pattern-policy (:150-158) selects by :precedence and :then over one flat collection, with grain carried as data in the guard. Dropping the field means the runner has nothing to check."}]
   :interim :third-grain-value
   :why-interim "It extends a mechanism that is already load-checked in every existing guard rather than introducing a second one, and the cost is honest and stated: the :policy conjunct is not built. The six patterns authored by slice (b) declare their grain in prose (\"**Policy grain.**\" as the first words of each + context:, matching the play-grain patterns' existing convention) and no code claims to enforce it."
   :changes-it "If slice (c)'s restated types make the grain recoverable from the rule's type at the point where rules are selected, the field becomes redundant and this should be re-run."
   :veto "Joe's. Nothing executable depends on it yet."}

  {:id :o4-under-a-temperament-then
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :LA1
   :question "Slice (a) open question 3: does law O4 survive a temperament pattern whose THEN emits precedence? O4 says precedence is recorded on the cascade as a collection-level field, \"not derived from the patterns\" -- and a temperament is a pattern."
   :grounds "P-validated-R5.md:502 states O4 and, in the same sentence, already calls the conflict-resolution order \"the temperament\". The argument O4 rests on is at p4ng/app-snatch.tex:117-126: the classical production-system answer is specificity, and it fails here because an-unmodelled-response-stops-the-line has the FEWEST conjuncts of any acting pattern and must be consulted first -- \"a commitment about the collection rather than a property of any member of it, and there is nowhere in a pattern to record it.\""
   :arms
   [{:arm :narrow-o4-to-the-ordered-patterns
     :form "O4 reads: precedence is not derived from the patterns IT ORDERS. A policy-grain rule may WRITE the field; it does not compute it from the members' contents."
     :cost "The law's wording has to carry the qualifier, and a reader who checks only the words \"not derived from the patterns\" will read a temperament pattern as a violation. It also means O4 no longer forbids everything it looks like it forbids."}
    {:arm :o4-forbids-it
     :form "a pattern may not emit precedence; temperaments stay outside the library"
     :cost "It forbids exactly the thing LA1 was opened to do, and it would leave the two temperaments that exist as Clojure literals (playout_snatch.clj:160,165) permanently unreachable from the collection."}
    {:arm :drop-o4
     :form "let precedence be derived after all"
     :cost "Gives up the app-snatch argument above, which is a measured finding and not a stipulation. Nothing has been shown to derive a correct order."}]
   :interim :narrow-o4-to-the-ordered-patterns
