  {:id :cascade-delta-fold-direction
   :status :interim-adopted
   :at "2026-09-02"
   :raised-by :LA4
   :question ":LA4's acceptance says \"attach-config extended to fold a cascade's deltas in PRECEDENCE order -- merge-deep is last-wins (cyber.clj:193-200), so the test that precedence decides a key collision is the acceptance test for O4 in this domain.\" It fixes THAT precedence decides the collision and does not fix WHICH member wins: the most precedent, or the least. The two readings disagree on a live number -- library/ants/pheromone-trail-tuner.flexiarg:11 writes :efe :lambda :info 0.5 and white-space-scout.flexiarg:13 writes 0.6, and the constructed cascade orders the tuner ahead of the scout."
   :arms
   [{:arm :most-precedent-wins
     :form "fold the `ordered` list BACKWARDS: `(apply merge-deep (reverse (map delta ordered)))`, so the least precedent member writes first and the most precedent member is merge-deep's last writer."
     :cost "The fold runs the precedence list in the opposite direction from the way the list is read, so the code has to say why in a docstring rather than being obvious from its shape. A reader who takes the acceptance's two clauses literally -- fold in precedence order, merge-deep is last-wins -- will expect the other answer and has to be told."}
    {:arm :last-in-precedence-order-wins
     :form "`(apply merge-deep (map delta ordered))` -- the literal reading of the acceptance sentence."
     :cost "It makes `attach` and `fire` read one field two ways. checks/find_organise.clj:398 `fire` sorts the rules by the cascade's precedence and takes the FIRST whose THEN yields, so precedence 1 is the rule that decides; under this arm precedence 1 would be the delta that LOSES every collision it is in. P-validated-R5's own argument for O4 (p4ng/app-snatch.tex:117-126) is that an-unmodelled-response-stops-the-line must be CONSULTED FIRST -- consulted first is what precedence means there."}
    {:arm :refuse-to-resolve
     :form "throw on a contended key; require the cascade to carry an explicit per-key resolution before it can be attached."
     :cost "futon2/holes/cascade-ants.edn:148 says composition needs a resolution rule and that last-write-wins is not one. This arm agrees and then supplies no rule, so NO cascade over library/ants can be attached at all, including the one the constructor built -- the row's second acceptance clause becomes unrunnable. It also puts the resolution somewhere other than the precedence field, which is what O4 says it must not be."}]
   :interim :most-precedent-wins
   :why-interim "It is the only arm under which `attach-cascade-config` and `fire` read `:precedence` the same way, and one field with two readings inside one system is the defect, not the inconvenience of an explanatory docstring. :refuse-to-resolve is not a resolution rule; it is the absence of one, restated as a guard. The direction is checked rather than documented: futon2/test/ants/cyber_test.clj `precedence-decides-a-key-collision` asserts 0.5 under one order and 0.6 under the other, with :members and :authored-order held fixed so precedence is the only difference, and `cascade-contentions` derives :won-by from the folded value rather than from position, so a wrong direction shows up as a mismatch instead of being restated as the intent."
   :changes-it "An authored cascade whose own THEN states a resolution rule for a contended key. That would move the resolution from the precedence field to the pattern text, and O4's reading would have to be revisited with it (`decisions.edn :o4-under-a-temperament-then` is the neighbouring question). Also: any consumer other than `fire` that reads `:precedence` in the opposite sense -- that would mean the field already carries two meanings and the choice would be between them rather than for one."
   :veto "Joe's. The undo is one `reverse` in futon2/src/ants/cyber.clj `cascade-delta` and the two flipped assertions in cyber_test.clj."}

